--  Keyless loader - Shadow X Hub
--  One file for every script that is served WITHOUT a key.
--  Change the three lines under CONFIG and nothing else.
--
--  !! ASCII ONLY IN THIS FILE. NO THAI, NO BOX DRAWING, NO EMOJI. !!
--
--  Not because keyless needs Luraph, but because you will eventually paste
--  this into Junkie's Script Protected slot (see WHERE TO PUT THIS below) and
--  Luraph reads the source with a single byte code page. Any byte above 127
--  kills it with
--      "No mapping for the Unicode character exists in the target multi-byte code page"
--  and the obfuscated output then fails silently in game: no error, no print,
--  nothing runs. Keeping this file ASCII from the start means you never have
--  to remember the rule on the day you actually need it.
--
--  WHAT KEYLESS BUYS YOU, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT
--    buys      the source is not in a public repo, and the GitHub token never
--              leaves the Worker
--    does not  secrecy. Whoever holds this loader can read the URL in it and
--              fetch the same source from a browser. That is inherent: the
--              loader has to reach the Worker, so it has to carry the address.
--
--  WHERE TO PUT THIS  (two ways, pick by how much you care)
--    1. Hand out  loadstring(game:HttpGet("<ENDPOINT>?s=<SCRIPT>"))()  directly.
--       Simplest. Nothing to host. The address is public, and that is fine
--       when the script is meant to be free anyway.
--    2. Put this file in Junkie's Script Protected slot with a dummy keyless
--       service (getgenv().SCRIPT_KEY = "KEYLESS"). Junkie obfuscates it, so
--       TOKEN below is not sitting in plain sight, and the Worker then refuses
--       anyone who guesses  ?s=<SCRIPT>  without it.
--       Still not secrecy - an obfuscated loader can be unwrapped - but it
--       stops the one attack that actually happens: someone who found the
--       endpoint in another script trying script ids until one answers.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------
--  CONFIG
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------
local ENDPOINT = "https://dry-sunset-f296.mr-personnel801.workers.dev/"

--  Must match GH_PATH_<SCRIPT> in the Worker, upper case.
local SCRIPT = "AFTERNIGHT"

--  Leave "" when KEYLESS_<SCRIPT> is set to 1 in the Worker.
--  Set it to the same random string when KEYLESS_<SCRIPT> holds one.
local TOKEN = "pak09ju9py713e1ue09jqwiodfnqbahfvi-ADoqkwif09qu89y12heuqwfpifj0pq8u"

--  Ask the Worker to lift top level locals (Luau caps them at 200 per chunk).
--  Project Afternight has 93, so it does not need this. Turn it on only for a
--  script that actually fails to compile with "too many local variables".
local LIFT = false

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------
--  TWO STEP HANDSHAKE - nothing to configure here
--
--  When CHALLENGE_<SCRIPT> is on in the Worker, the first reply is not the
--  script. It is a small stub that proves the request came from a Roblox
--  client that is actually running, and only then asks for the real payload.
--
--  This file needs no change for that, and that is the whole point of the
--  design: a loader only fetches, compiles and runs whatever came back, so
--  the stub can stand in for the script. The handshake can therefore be
--  turned on, changed or turned off from the Cloudflare dashboard while
--  every loader already sitting in a Junkie slot keeps working untouched.
--
--  !! It raises the bar. It does not close the door.
--  setclipboard(game:HttpGet(url)) now returns the stub instead of the
--  source, so copying one line is no longer enough - but anyone who hooks
--  HttpGet or loadstring still gets everything. That is not a hole to be
--  patched later; the script has to arrive as text a compiler can read, so
--  whoever can run it can read it. Do not write code here that assumes
--  otherwise.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------

